>> once somebody is out placed that we're going to get them back, right? Yeah, because, thanks for bringing that up Robert because just so that anybody who does not know a lot about that process.

00:31:57.470 --> 00:31:59.790
No district does it lightly, as you can imagine,

00:32:00.715 --> 00:32:05.115
because you know we want the least restrictive environment for a student but we also

00:32:05.355 --> 00:32:10.555
want to service their needs, the best that we can in house if it's possible.

00:32:10.795 --> 00:32:12.635
So before a child

00:32:12.875 --> 00:32:13.595
is

00:32:13.915 --> 00:32:16.075
placed in a program that's not our program.

00:32:16.610 --> 00:32:18.690
Have already gone through a variety of things.

00:32:19.490 --> 00:32:22.530
First, there are tiered supports within the classroom.

00:32:22.770 --> 00:32:28.770
Can the classroom teacher and potentially a special education teacher or specialist manage to

00:32:29.490 --> 00:32:30.370
provide services?

00:32:30.835 --> 00:32:34.115
If not, then oftentimes it's moved to the next level.

00:32:34.515 --> 00:32:40.355
Potentially testing is done to see if maybe we're not meeting the needs for an identified

00:32:40.355 --> 00:32:41.155
purpose

00:32:41.555 --> 00:32:45.635
with that student sometimes then that's when you would see them adding a pair of professional.

00:32:46.170 --> 00:32:57.690
Maybe that is the way to go, the strategy to utilize. So there are these steps along the way all the way up until we realize at a certain point that we cannot service the needs of a child.

00:32:58.205 --> 00:33:07.245
And that's when we then have to look for another place for their needs to be serviced. So, yeah, Robert, once they are placed out,

00:33:08.125 --> 00:33:08.765
oftentimes,

00:33:09.085 --> 00:33:16.510
that's where they wind up because we've already gone through all those other steps and determined that we couldn't meet their needs. But

00:33:16.830 --> 00:33:17.870
annually,

00:33:17.950 --> 00:33:20.990
